Spark failure


2 AM. Production alert. 11TB pipeline stopped mid-run. Root cause: one line of PySpark I wrote. Here's the full story.

Slack at 1:58 AM: "Pipeline FAILED — stage 4/9. Board deck due 8 AM." Four engineers on Zoom in minutes. Six downstream teams blocked. The silence on that call was the loudest thing I've ever heard.

Spark executor OOM at stage 4. Exit code 137. Nothing had changed — same config, same volume. Or so I thought.

Git blame at 3:17 AM. My commit. I had added .repartition(2000) right before a wide aggregation to "improve parallelism." It forced a full 11TB shuffle before the aggregation even started. Memory never recovered.

✗ df.repartition(2000).groupBy("region").agg(sum("revenue"))
✓ df.groupBy("region").agg(sum("revenue")) # let Spark decide

"I had tested it — on 50GB of clean dev data. Not 11TB of messy production records."

Deleted the line. Restarted from checkpoint at stage 3. Kept the old DAG version live as rollback — stale data beats no data. Pipeline finished at 6:14 AM. Board deck landed at 7:47 AM. Full RCA written by 10 AM.

What I learned:

1. Test with production schema, not clean dev data. Same skew, same nulls, 1–5% real sample.
2. .repartition() before a wide shuffle doubles your cost. Use .coalesce() after, or trust AQE.
3. Checkpoint placement is a design decision. We got lucky it existed at stage 3.

Built a monitoring framework that week — shuffle size alerts, executor memory watermarks, stage duration drift, partition skew detection. It's caught 4 repartition-class errors in 14 months since.

The best data engineers aren't the ones who never fail.

They fail once, fix the conditions that allowed it, and make sure the whole team is safer because it happened. That 2 AM call is why I build differently now. Not a scar — a standard.

